    <div class="break" style="margin-top:88px">
      <figure id="cb-iwcen" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3 id="iwcen-title">Eighty-eight towns, one index</h3>
            <div class="sub">CPI-IW by centre, year-on-year, per cent, {PIWC.long}, base 2016, sorted: every industrial centre the Labour Bureau prices, from mill towns to tea gardens</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-iwcen">–</div><div class="c">spread across centres, {PIWC.short}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-iwcen"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Labour Bureau, via RBI DBIE (report 1168), base 2016=100, each centre is priced in its own markets with its own basket, dashed rule: All India; a centre is a town, not a state, so a state can appear several times</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://labourbureau.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each dot is a town where the Bureau sends price collectors to the markets an industrial worker uses. The centres are chosen for their factories and mines, not to cover a state, so read them as places: Jamshedpur, Coimbatore, Kolkata. Dearness allowance is paid on the All-India figure, so a worker in a centre well above the dashed rule is falling behind on an allowance that averages the country.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {PIWC.stamp}</span>
            <p>The spread runs from <b id="iwc-top">–</b> to <b id="iwc-bot">–</b>: <b id="iwc-spread">–</b> end to end. <b id="iwc-above">–</b> of <b id="iwc-n">–</b> centres ran above the All-India rate of <b id="iwc-ai">–</b>, and <b id="iwc-neg">–</b> saw prices fall over the year.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:88px">
      <figure id="cb-wage" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3 id="wage-title">Did the farm wage keep up with farm prices?</h3>
            <div class="sub">Daily wage of a man working as a general agricultural labourer, All India, growth on a year earlier, per cent, against CPI-AL inflation (base 1986-87), <span id="wage-span">–</span>: both are Labour Bureau series</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-wage">–</div><div class="c">wage, {PWG.short}, rupees a day</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-wage">
            <button class="chip" data-key="wage"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Wage growth, general farm labourer</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="al"><i style="background:#C47A16"></i>CPI-AL inflation</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-wage"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Labour Bureau, Wage Rates in Rural India (RBI DBIE report 664) and CPI-AL (eSankhyiki), wage growth computed from the published daily wage; the occupation list was redrawn in November 2013, so the line starts a year later; ends where the old CPI-AL base ends, May 2025</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://labourbureau.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>A price index only tells half the story for a wage earner. The other half is the wage. The Labour Bureau collects both for rural India: what a day's work pays in each occupation, and what the farm labourer's household pays for its basket. When the blue line runs above the gold one, a day's wage bought a little more than it did a year before; below it, a little less.</p>
            <p>The wage is the average man's daily wage across the Bureau's sample villages, for the general agricultural labourer: watering, weeding, the work anyone can be hired for. It is not what any one worker earns, and it says nothing about how many days of work there were.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {PWG.stamp}</span>
            <p>A day of general farm labour paid <b id="wg-now">–</b> in {PWG.long}, up from <b id="wg-first">–</b> in {PWG.firstLong}. Over the <b id="wg-n">–</b> months both series cover, the wage grew faster than farm prices in <b id="wg-above">–</b>; on average, wage growth ran <b id="wg-gap">–</b> above farm-price inflation. That is a slow gain in what a day's work buys, and it says nothing about the number of days.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
