  <!-- § 14 misread -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">14</span><h2>How to misread this number</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Inflation fell, so prices fell."</h3>
          <p>No. Falling inflation means prices are rising more slowly: disinflation. At 2%, the basket still costs 2% more than a year ago. Only a negative print means falling prices, and that happens to single items and groups (vegetables, pulses) far more often than to the whole basket.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"My inflation is the national inflation."</h3>
          <p>Your basket is not the average basket. The weights are a snapshot of average spending in a survey year; they describe nobody precisely. A family spending 60 per cent on food lives in a different index, and so does a family in a different state: the state table in §11 shows the same month reading very differently from one state to the next.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"One month tells a story."</h3>
          <p>Vegetables can move the headline by a full point in a single month and reverse the next. Month-to-month moves are mostly seasonal noise; three consecutive months is the minimum unit of meaning, and twelve is better.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The base doesn't matter."</h3>
          <p>Every y/y figure is a ratio to the same month a year ago. A spike then flatters now; a collapse then haunts now. Before reacting to any print, ask what the denominator was doing.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">v.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Core is the real inflation."</h3>
          <p>Core excludes what poor households spend most on. It is watched because it is stable and policy-responsive, not because it is truer. For the cost of living as lived, the headline is the honest number.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">vi.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"CPI and WPI contradict each other."</h3>
          <p>They survey different terrain: retail consumers versus the factory gate, with different baskets and different fuels. Divergence of five points is normal. Both are correct; show them as separate series, never as a conflict to resolve.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">vii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Dearness allowance follows the CPI."</h3>
          <p>It follows a different CPI. Central government dearness allowance is set from the CPI for industrial workers (§13), a Labour Bureau index of a factory worker's basket in 88 towns, averaged over twelve months. It can run a point or more away from the national CPI in either direction, and it rebases on its own timetable.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">viii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Rural wages rose 7%, so labourers are 7% better off."</h3>
          <p>Only if prices stood still. A wage rise is a gain only above the labourer's own inflation, and that is CPI-AL, not the national CPI. Even then the daily rate says nothing about how many days of work there were in the month, which is where rural incomes most often fall.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

